I'm not particularly good at Nethack.  I played Hack a few times on my
faithful
old Atari ST sometime around 1990, and then never thought about the game
again until about two years ago, when I picked up a copy of Nethack for my
PC.  I floundered around for a while, getting slaughtered on about DL4,
before I started reading various beginners' guides and other hints and tips
(including the odd spolier, slapped wrist...).  Now I can get barbarians and
valkyries down to Mines End reasonably often.

Every now and then, with a mixture of luck and less-than-usual stupidity, I
have a wizard, archaeologist or monk who does equally well.  My best
performances were the two barbarians I got to the bottom of the mines and
back, and down to the "Big Room" in the dungeon proper.  Anyway, enough
scene-setting; let me tell you about my latest annoying death....

This morning's neutral valkyrie was looking rather promising, by my own
not-very-great standards.  She was up to 110 MaxHP, had managed to pick up
some half decent gear in the Mines to get her AC down to 0, and Richard
Parker had picked off enough jackals, newts and grid bugs on the upper
levels
to become a reasonably handy housecat.  I like to tackle the mines before
going too deep in the dungeon proper, especially with a valk - they're
sufficiently strong and well-equippied to reach reach Mine Town
without problems, and they can normally pick up helmet, shoes and maybe
armour on the way.  The Mine Town altar wasn't co-aligned so she was still
wielding her entry-level long sword.  Richard Parker swiped the three
candles from the lighting shop (the shopkeeper had mostly mimics and lamps,
unfortunately) and mistress and pussycat descended further.

Next to the downward staircase on level 9, she steps on a polymorph trap and
turns into a baby orange dragon.  Could have been a lot worse - especially
since she's not wearing armour, so all that gets destroyed is her oilskin
cloak.  She heads down to level 10 and sets about the local gnomes,
dwarves, hobbits &c.

She's down to around 30 HP (from a baby D max of about 50) when she's
grabbed by an owlbear.  She tries to hit back, but after a couple of turns
she returns to human form, wearing no protective gear and not wielding a
weapon.  She's got nothing she can use to engrave quickly (is it possible to
engrave whilst grabbed by an owlbear?) so her first move is to equip her +3
shield.  Trying to run away reveals that the owlbear has grabbed her (alas
for her shredded oilskin cloak!).  She searches in her bag for anything
which might let her get away, but has no ? or / of Teleport.  Indeed, she
has nothing positively identified that looks like a ticket outta there.  So
she sets about the owlbear with the / of striking she picked up earlier,
desparately hoping that Richard Parker's doing his bit on her behalf as
well.  When she's down below 20HP, she calls for divine intervention.  The
owlbear "loosens its grip for a moment" (IIRC) but not enough to let her get
away.  She resorts to breaking her /oS but it's spent, as is the other
unidentified wand she also breaks in two.  After a couple more turns the
owlbear has choked the life out of her and all that's left is DYWYPI and a
debut post to RGRN...

Did she do anything obviously stupid?  Perhaps she brought it on herself by
stomping around at level 10 with only a couple of dozen HP.  Perhaps she
should have made more effort to identify more stuff before venturing so low.
Perhaps it's foolhardy not to have some means of engraving in one turn.
Anything else?

BTW, something else she saw struck her as rather out of the ordinary.  On
two separate occasions, she dispatched run-of-the-mill Gnome Lords, who both
turned out to be wearing an " of Life Saving!  If only she'd been able to
(a) see 'em and (b) steal 'em, things might have gone rather better for her.
Oh well.

Thanks for all the helpful stuff I've been reading here over the last few
days.  I now appreciate the assistance of a well-developed pet, especially
when playing less-strong characters.  Oh, and I've given up quaffing from
fountains.  The frequency of "killed by a water moccasin" in my logfile is
scary....

Until next time.

